<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tamna</h1><p class="st-meta">476 CE – 1105 CE (approx.) · capital Jeju</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The island kingdom of Jeju, which kept its own rulers for six centuries as a tributary of Korean kingdoms before Goryeo made it a county in 1105.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju</a> legend tells of three founders, ancestors of the Go, Yang and Bu clans, who emerged from holes in the ground at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samseonghyeol">Samseonghyeol</a> and married princesses who arrived by sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> in 476, when it sent tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island was ruled by hereditary lords with the titles seongju and wangja, drawn from the Go clan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> lived from fishing, diving and farming on volcanic soil and traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, sending envoys to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> court and to Japan in the 7th century. It paid tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a>, then to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> after 662, and to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a> from 938.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a> turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> into a county in 1105, ending its formal autonomy, though local lords kept their titles until the early <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/joseon">Joseon</a> period. The island was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju</a> in the early 13th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tamna.jpg" alt="Tamna" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamna_kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>476 CE – 1105 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jeju</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Jeju</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tamna Kingdom, Tangna, Takmora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=790&amp;focus=tamna" aria-label="Tamna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7912 962 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tamna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8232 1218L8208 1141M8232 1218L8260 1139M8232 1218L8154 1069"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8208 1141h0M8260 1139h0M8154 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8232 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8232 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jeju. <a href="/atlas?year=790&amp;focus=tamna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
